This isn't a webforum, it's Usenet. There are no moderators, and
nobody's in charge. (Rather, people use "killfiles" to simply not listen
to people they don't like; trolls typically get bored quickly when
nobody can see their posts. And individual news servers will have their
own protection against spambots.) There's such a thing as a cancel
message, that you can send out to try to delete a message, but most
news servers will ignore them without proof you're the original order,
and there's insufficient information in your message for anyone to
prove that they sent it. (This message has a Cancel-Lock header, which
is the hash of some random string that only my news provider knows, so
if I needed to cancel this message, I could provide the original
string as proof. But Google Groups, which you're apparently using to
post, doesn't add those.) So it'll just end up floating around until
it falls out of the news spools in a week or so. (And Google will
presumably archive both copies for ever; they do that.)

Google Groups is notorious for multiple-posting, by the way. (Also
delaying posts to everyone but other Google Groups users, then randomly
posting them several days later. Also not formatting messages correctly
by default.) Usenet regulars tend to look down on it as a result,
although it is still probably the most convenient of the news servers.

